  "summary": "Brass vessels of Mamluk Egypt and Syria crowded with silver-inlaid blazons, calligraphy and courtly scenes.",
  "description": "Mamluk metalworkers in Cairo and Aleppo raised brass candlesticks, basins, ewers and mosque lamps, then chased and inlaid them with silver and gold: roundels of musicians, hunters and polo players alternating with bands of thuluth calligraphy and the armorial blazons of emirs — a cup, a sword, an eagle. The Baptistère de Saint-Louis in the Louvre, signed by Muhammad ibn al-Zayn, is the most celebrated example. After the Ottoman conquest the style simplified into export wares for Europe and the Ottoman court.",
